  "worked_solution": "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=6X,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=4Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86X,3Y,4Z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,3\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $\u03d5\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5.$",
